      "section_type": "content",
      "id": 5,
      "title": "Physics: Wave Period and Frequency",
      "renderer": "wan_video",
      "narration": "Let's now ride the waves into physics. Waves are a fundamental mechanism for transferring energy. Think of ripples in a pond, the sound from a speaker, or the light from the sun—they all travel as waves. A crucial point is that waves move energy from one place to another, but the medium itself, like the water particles, doesn't travel along with the wave; it mostly just oscillates in place. To describe any wave, we use a few key properties. The wavelength, represented by the Greek letter lambda (λ), is the spatial length of one complete cycle of the wave—for instance, the distance from one crest to the next. The frequency, 'f', tells us how many complete wave cycles pass by a fixed point every second. It's measured in Hertz (Hz). A high-frequency wave has many cycles passing per second, while a low-frequency wave has fewer. The wave speed 'v' is simply how fast a point on the wave, like a crest, is traveling. These three properties are beautifully related by the simple formula: wave speed equals frequency times wavelength, or v = fλ. There's one more important concept: the period, 'T'. The period is the time it takes for one full wavelength to pass a point. It's the inverse of frequency. If a wave has a high frequency, many cycles pass per second, so the time for just one cycle, its period, must be very short. Thus, T = 1/f.",
