    {
      "section_type": "content",
      "id": 6,
      "title": "Chemistry: The Equilibrium Constant (Kc)",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Let's transition to chemistry and explore the concept of dynamic equilibrium. Many chemical reactions are not a one-way street. While reactants form products, the products can also react to re-form the original reactants. We call these 'reversible reactions'. Imagine a reaction where molecules A and B combine to form C and D. This is the forward reaction. At the same time, molecules C and D can break apart or react to create A and B again—that's the reverse reaction. Initially, when we only have reactants, the forward reaction is fast and the reverse reaction is non-existent. As products build up, the forward reaction slows down (since there are fewer reactants) and the reverse reaction speeds up. Eventually, the reaction reaches a state of equilibrium. This doesn't mean the reaction has stopped! It means the rate of the forward reaction has become exactly equal to the rate of the reverse reaction. Reactants are still forming products, and products are still forming reactants, but their overall concentrations in the mixture stop changing. To quantify this balance, we use the equilibrium constant, Kc. For a general reaction aA + bB is in equilibrium with cC + dD, where the small letters are the stoichiometric coefficients, Kc is a ratio. We take the concentrations of the products, [C] and [D], raised to the power of their coefficients, and divide them by the concentrations of the reactants, [A] and [B], also raised to their coefficients. This value, Kc, tells us about the position of the equilibrium. A large Kc means the reaction favors the products, while a small Kc means it favors the reactants.",
      "narration_segments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "text": "Let's transition to chemistry and explore the concept of dynamic equilibrium. Many chemical reactions are not a one-way street. While reactants form products, the products can also react to re-form the original reactants. We call these 'reversible reactions'.",
          "duration": 15
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "text": "Imagine a reaction where molecules A and B combine to form C and D. This is the forward reaction. At the same time, molecules C and D can break apart or react to create A and B again—that's the reverse reaction.",
          "duration": 13
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "text": "Initially, when we only have reactants, the forward reaction is fast and the reverse reaction is non-existent. As products build up, the forward reaction slows down (since there are fewer reactants) and the reverse reaction speeds up.",
          "duration": 14
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "text": "Eventually, the reaction reaches a state of equilibrium. This doesn't mean the reaction has stopped! It means the rate of the forward reaction has become exactly equal to the rate of the reverse reaction.",
          "duration": 12
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "text": "Reactants are still forming products, and products are still forming reactants, but their overall concentrations in the mixture stop changing. To quantify this balance, we use the equilibrium constant, Kc.",
          "duration": 13
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "text": "For a general reaction aA + bB is in equilibrium with cC + dD, where the small letters are the stoichiometric coefficients, Kc is a ratio. We take the concentrations of the products, [C] and [D], raised to the power of their coefficients, and divide them by the concentrations of the reactants, [A] and [B], also raised to their coefficients.",
          "duration": 20
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "text": "This value, Kc, tells us about the position of the equilibrium. A large Kc means the reaction favors the products, while a small Kc means it favors the reactants.",
          "duration": 9
        }
      ],
